# EP03 — What Happens to Your Body If You Get Struck by Lightning
# Narration tags like [S3] point to the sources below; they are stripped before the voice is made.
METER: SINCE STRIKE
CASE: CASE 03 / 300,000,000 V
SPRITE you | {you} standing, front view, arms relaxed, slightly nervous expression, looking up
SPRITE fried | {you} standing stiff with smoking hair sticking straight up, wide shocked eyes, tiny wisps of smoke, comedic
SPRITE bolt | a single jagged lightning bolt glowing white and yellow, vertical
SPRITE heart | a human heart glowing orange, simple anatomical line art
SPRITE cloud | a single dark storm cloud glowing with cyan outline and small orange flashes inside
SPRITE hat | a park ranger campaign hat with a burnt hole in it, glowing cyan line art
SPRITE golf | a golf club and a golf ball glowing cyan line art
# id | picture | tokens | caption | narration
# ---- HOOK ----
h01 | {you} standing in an open field with arms raised in shock, a huge jagged lightning bolt hitting the person's head from storm clouds above, white and yellow bolt, glowing outline | flash sparks hold hr=0 temp=36.9 o2=95 t=0.000s s=CRIT | 300,000,000 VOLTS | Three hundred million volts. Thirty thousand amps. Your wall socket gives you about one hundred and twenty volts. You just got the whole sky. [S1]
h02 | glowing human figure outline with a thin bright line of electricity running over the skin surface and a few lines going through the chest, cyan and yellow | glow zoomin | 5x hotter than the Sun | The air around the bolt heats to around fifty thousand degrees Fahrenheit. That's about five times hotter than the surface of the Sun. [S2]
h03 | - | spr:fried@540,1010,0.95,on,shake dust hr=88 o2=97 s=WARN | 9 in 10 SURVIVE | And yet, about nine out of ten people struck by lightning survive. [S3]
h04 | =h03 | zoomin | | That sounds like the good news. It isn't, really. Because what lightning does to the survivors is the strangest part of this whole story. Let's go back thirty minutes, to the moment you made a terrible decision.
# ---- T-MINUS 30 MINUTES ----
s01 | {you} on a golf course holding a golf club, a dark storm cloud far away on the horizon, sunny patch above the person | panl hr=72 temp=36.8 o2=98 t=-30min s=OK | | It's a summer afternoon. You're on a golf course. There's a dark cloud way off on the horizon. It isn't even raining where you are.
s02 | =s01 | zoomin | | You hear a low rumble of thunder. You think, eh, it's miles away. Three more holes.
s03 | a glowing map with a storm cloud on one side and a lightning bolt striking far away from it on the other side, a long dotted arrow between them | glow hold | Bolts from the blue: 10–15 miles | Here's the problem. Lightning often strikes more than three miles from the centre of a storm, far outside the rain. So-called bolts from the blue can land ten to fifteen miles away. [S5]
s04 | =s03 | panr | Hear thunder? You're in range. | The National Weather Service puts it bluntly. If you can hear thunder, even a distant rumble, you are already in danger. [S5, S11]
s05 | - | spr:golf@760,960,0.35,drop,bob spr:you@460,1010,0.95,on,breathe | | And standing in an open field, as the tallest thing around, holding a long stick, is exactly how you make yourself interesting.
# ---- HOW IT FINDS YOU ----
s06 | glowing lightning bolt hitting a tall lone tree, and a small glowing person standing under the tree getting a small side spark, cyan and yellow | flash sparks zoomin t=-5min | Side flash | There are five main ways lightning gets you. A direct strike. A side flash, where it hits a tree and jumps to the person sheltering under it. Standing under a tree is the second leading cause of lightning casualties. [S4, S5]
s07 | glowing lightning bolt hitting the ground, with glowing electricity spreading outward along the ground in rings, small glowing figures standing nearby | glow hold | Ground current: most casualties | Ground current, where the energy spreads out through the ground. That one causes the most lightning deaths and injuries, because it covers a much bigger area. [S4]
s08 | a glowing house with a glowing person inside standing at a sink with a running tap, electricity running along the water pipes, cyan line art | panl | | Conduction, through wires and pipes. That's how people get hurt indoors, in the shower, or on a corded phone. And streamers, little sparks of electricity rising off the ground to meet the bolt. [S4]
s09 | =h01 | hold | | Today, you go for the classic. A direct strike. The rarest kind, but potentially the deadliest. [S4]
# ---- 0.000 SECONDS: THE STRIKE ----
s10 | =h02 | flash sparks glow zoomin hr=0 o2=95 t=0.000s s=CRIT | FLASHOVER | The bolt hits you, and something surprising happens. A lot of the current doesn't go through you. It races along the outside, just over your skin. This is called flashover. [S4]
s11 | =s10 | hold | | That's one reason lightning burns are usually not as bad as you'd expect. The real damage is done by the part of the current that goes inside, through your heart and your nervous system. [S4, S6]
s12 | - | spr:heart@540,760,0.55,on,shake alert | ASYSTOLE | Your heart stops. Doctors call it asystole. The electrical rhythm that runs your heart just, resets. [S7]
s13 | glowing human brain stem and lungs with a dim breathing center, lungs not moving, orange alarm glow | glow alert zoomin hr=0 o2=80 t=+10s | RESPIRATORY ARREST | And the area of your brain that controls breathing gets knocked out too. You stop breathing. [S7]
s14 | - | spr:heart@540,760,0.55,none,breathe beat hr=64 o2=70 t=+30s s=CRIT | Heart restarts | Now here's the twist. Your heart has its own built-in pacemaker, and the asystole may resolve by itself, fairly quickly. Your heart can start beating again on its own. [S7]
s15 | =s13 | glow hold hr=70 o2=55 t=+60s | Breathing stays off | But breathing often stays switched off for longer. So your heart is back, beating away, pumping blood with no oxygen in it. That's what can kill you, a few minutes after you survived the actual lightning. [S7]
s16 | a glowing person kneeling and doing CPR chest compressions on a glowing person lying on the grass, rain falling | rain pop zoomin hr=96 o2=88 t=+90s s=WARN | SAFE TO TOUCH | So if you ever see this happen, do something. Call emergency services, start CPR, use a defibrillator if there's one. And no, the victim doesn't stay electrified. The human body does not store electricity. It's safe to touch them. [S6, S8]
s17 | =s16 | hold | | Imagine dying because everyone was too scared to touch you. That's how much this myth matters. [S6]
# ---- +1 MINUTE: THE AFTERMATH ----
s18 | a glowing person lying on grass with pale bluish legs and arms, faint glow, unable to move, worried eyes | glow zoomin hr=104 o2=96 t=+5min | Keraunoparalysis | You wake up. But you can't move your legs. They're pale, maybe bluish, and nobody can find a pulse in them. [S7]
s19 | =s18 | hold | Usually gone in hours | It's called keraunoparalysis, a temporary paralysis caused by lightning. Terrifying. But it usually goes away within a few hours. [S7]
s20 | close up of a glowing human ear with a small orange crack in the eardrum, little sound waves broken, cyan line art | glow panl | | Your ears are ringing. The blast can rupture your eardrums. [S7]
s21 | close-up of a human back and shoulder glowing cyan, with a large bright orange branching fern-like pattern spreading across the skin like tree branches or a feather, a lightning tattoo | glow zoomin t=+1h | LICHTENBERG FIGURES | And on your skin, there's something beautiful. A pattern like a fern, or the branches of a tree, spreading across your body. They're called Lichtenberg figures. [S9]
s22 | =s21 | hold | Fade in about a day | They usually disappear within about twenty-four hours. Lightning gives you a tattoo, and then takes it back. [S9]
s23 | - | spr:fried@540,1010,0.95,on,breathe dust hr=88 temp=37.0 o2=98 t=+1day s=WARN | | So you're alive. You can walk. The burns are mild. The doctors run their scans, and the scans come back normal. You go home. Lucky you. Right?
# ---- +1 WEEK: THE INVISIBLE INJURY ----
s24 | glowing human brain with flickering broken connections, some lines dim and some sparking orange, cyan line art | glow zoomin t=+1week | Mainly a nervous system injury | Here's what most people get wrong. Lightning is mainly an injury to your nervous system. Your brain and your nerves. [S6]
s25 | {you} sitting on a sofa holding the head with a pained expression, little glowing scribbles above the head, a glowing clock showing late night | panr | | You get headaches. Nausea. You struggle to learn new information, and to pull up old information. You can't remember names, or what you were supposed to be doing at work. [S6]
s26 | {you} lying in bed wide awake staring at the ceiling, a glowing clock, dark room | hold | | Your sleep goes strange too. Sometimes you sleep way too much at first, and later only two or three hours at a time. [S6]
s27 | a glowing hospital scanner machine with a glowing brain image on a screen that looks normal with a green check glow | panl | Scans: "normal" | And the worst part? The x-rays and MRIs come back normal. Like a concussion, the problem is in how your brain works, not in how it looks on a picture. [S6]
s28 | {you} standing alone while a group of glowing friends walk away in the distance, the person looks sad, cyan line art | zoomin | | Your friends see the same person on the outside. They don't understand why you're different. Some stop calling. Some survivors pull away out of embarrassment. [S6]
s29 | =s28 | hold t=+1year | ~75% with lasting problems | Of the people who survive a lightning strike, about three in four have health problems that last. [S7]
s30 | close up of a glowing human eye with a cloudy orange glowing lens inside, cyan line art | glow zoomin | Cataracts, even a year later | And some injuries show up late. Cataracts, a clouding of the lens in your eye, can appear more than a year after an otherwise uneventful recovery. [S7]
# ---- THE MAN STRUCK SEVEN TIMES ----
s31 | - | spr:hat@540,760,0.45,drop,bob dust t=7_STRIKES | THE HUMAN LIGHTNING ROD | Now, if you're thinking, once in a lifetime, fine. Let me introduce Roy Sullivan. [S10]
s32 | a glowing park ranger man in a ranger uniform and campaign hat standing in a forest, mountains behind, cyan line art | panr | Roy Sullivan, park ranger | Roy was a park ranger at Shenandoah National Park in Virginia. Between nineteen forty-two and nineteen seventy-seven, he was struck by lightning seven times. Guinness World Records recognises him as the person struck the most recorded times. [S10]
s33 | a tall wooden fire lookout tower on a mountain top with a lightning bolt hitting it, a small glowing man running away from it, cyan and yellow | flash zoomin | 1942: lost his eyebrows | Strike one, nineteen forty-two, at a new fire lookout tower that had no lightning rod. It burned off his eyebrows and eyelashes, and set his hair on fire. [S10]
s34 | =s32 | hold | | Later strikes hit him in his own front yard, and inside a ranger station. His hair caught fire again. And again. [S10]
s35 | - | spr:cloud@700,420,0.5,on,float spr:you@420,1010,0.9,walkr,bob | | By the end, he believed storm clouds were following him. Once, he saw a cloud, tried to run away from it, and got struck anyway. He started carrying a can of water around, in case his hair caught fire. [S10]
s36 | =s35 | hold | | People started avoiding him, scared they'd get hit just by standing nearby. Which, if you think about it, is the loneliest possible side effect of being lucky.
# ---- HOW TO NOT BE ROY ----
s37 | a glowing car and a glowing house side by side in the rain under a storm cloud, both glowing safe green, cyan line art | rain panl hr=72 temp=36.8 t=SAFETY s=OK | | So here's how to keep your vitals boring. There's no safe place outdoors in a thunderstorm. The only properly safe places are a substantial building or a hard topped metal vehicle. [S5, S11]
s38 | =s37 | zoomin | Crouching doesn't help. | Crouching down doesn't make you safe. Lying flat makes ground current worse. And don't shelter under a tree. Just get inside. [S5]
s39 | a tall glowing skyscraper being struck by several lightning bolts at its tip, night city, cyan and yellow | flash panr | Empire State Building: ~23 hits a year | And lightning absolutely does strike the same place twice. The Empire State Building gets hit around twenty-three times a year. [S5]
s40 | glowing statistics bar chart without text, one small orange bar and one tall cyan bar, with a small lightning bolt icon | glow hold | US: ~27 deaths/yr (2009–2018) | The good news. In the United States, lightning deaths have dropped a lot, from about forty-three a year on average over three decades, to about twenty-seven a year in the decade to twenty eighteen. Your odds of being struck in a given year are around one in a million. [S3]
s41 | =s40 | zoomin | Lifetime odds: ~1 in 15,300 | But over a whole lifetime, it's about one in fifteen thousand. And for every person struck, around ten people around them are affected too. [S3]
# ---- OUTRO ----
s42 | - | spr:you@540,1010,0.95,on,breathe dust | | So, lightning doesn't usually kill you. It stops your heart, switches off your breathing, paints you like a fern, scrambles your memory, and then sends you home with a clean scan. Survival, it turns out, is complicated.
s43 | - | spr:you@540,1010,0.95,none,breathe | | When thunder roars, go indoors. Not after the next hole. Now.
s44 | - | endcard spr:you@330,980,0.8,none,breathe t=NEXT | NEXT: 30 DAYS OF FAST FOOD | Next week, you eat nothing but fast food for thirty days. We'll check your liver every week, and the scariest result shows up a year after you stop. See you then.
# ---- SOURCES ----
# [S1] NOAA National Weather Service, "Lightning: power": a typical flash is ~300 million volts and ~30,000 amps; household current 120 V / 15 A. https://www.weather.gov/safety/lightning-power
# [S2] NOAA National Weather Service, "How hot is lightning?": air heated to ~50,000 °F, five times hotter than the surface of the Sun. https://www.weather.gov/safety/lightning-temperature
# [S3] NOAA National Weather Service, "How dangerous is lightning?": ~10% of people struck are killed, ~90% left with various degrees of disability; average 43 US deaths/yr (1989–2018), 27/yr (2009–2018); odds 1 in 1,222,000 per year, 1 in 15,300 in a lifetime; ~10 people affected for every 1 struck; cardiac arrest at the time of injury. https://www.weather.gov/safety/lightning-odds
# [S4] NOAA National Weather Service, "How lightning strikes people": direct strike (flashover over the skin surface, portion through cardiovascular/nervous systems), side flash, ground current (most deaths and injuries), conduction (most indoor casualties), streamers. https://www.weather.gov/safety/lightning-struck
# [S5] NOAA National Weather Service, "Lightning myths": strikes >3 miles from the storm, bolts from the blue 10–15 miles; strikes the same place repeatedly (Empire State Building ~23 times a year); under a tree is the second leading cause of casualties; crouching doesn't make you safer; lying flat increases ground-current risk; the body does not store electricity. https://www.weather.gov/safety/lightning-myths
# [S6] NOAA National Weather Service, "Medical aspects of lightning" (Dr Mary Ann Cooper): primarily a nervous-system injury; serious burns seldom occur; headaches, nausea, memory and learning problems, sleep changes; scans often "normal"; social isolation. https://www.weather.gov/safety/lightning-medical
# [S7] Wikipedia, "Lightning injury": ~10% lethal; initial asystole and respiratory arrest, asystole may resolve spontaneously while respiratory arrest is more prolonged; ~75% of survivors have ongoing problems (e.g., cataracts, hearing loss); keraunoparalysis (pulselessness, pallor/cyanosis, limb paralysis) usually resolves within hours; ruptured eardrums; cataracts may develop more than a year later. https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lightning_injury
# [S8] NOAA National Weather Service, "How lightning strikes people" (call 911, start CPR, use an AED). https://www.weather.gov/safety/lightning-struck
# [S9] Wikipedia, "Lichtenberg figure": fern-like patterns on the skin of lightning victims (keraunographic markings) that typically disappear in about 24 hours. https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lichtenberg_figure
# [S10] Wikipedia, "Roy Sullivan": Shenandoah National Park ranger; struck seven times 1942–1977; Guinness record; 1942 fire lookout tower without a lightning rod, eyebrows/eyelashes burned off and hair set alight; strikes in his front yard (1970) and in a ranger station (1972); believed clouds followed him; carried a can of water; avoided by people. https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roy_Sullivan
# [S11] NOAA National Weather Service, "Understanding lightning" ("if you hear thunder ... you are already in danger") and lightning safety pages (safe shelter = substantial building or hard-topped metal vehicle). https://www.weather.gov/safety/lightning-science-overview
# [S12] Ritenour AE, Morton MJ, McManus JG, Barillo DJ, Cancio LC. Lightning injury: a review. Burns 2008;34(5):585–594 (background review used for cross-checking). https://doi.org/10.1016/j.burns.2007.11.006
# ---- FACT-CHECK ----
# - The 50,000 °F figure is the temperature of the air in the lightning channel (NWS), not of the victim. Narration says "the air around the bolt" — OK.
# - "Your wall socket gives you about 120 volts" is the US figure (NWS); much of the world uses 220–240 V. Consider adding "in the US" if the audience skews non-US.
# - Roy Sullivan died in 1983 by suicide (Wikipedia). We deliberately don't mention or joke about it.
# - Survival framing: "about nine out of ten survive" (NWS 10% fatality).
# - "Respiratory arrest is what can kill a few minutes later" is our explanation of S7's asystole vs prolonged respiratory arrest; consistent with S3 (cardiac arrest, delayed deaths from brain damage).
